The reducer: line 22 — `!(action.type === 'view' && !state.follow)` — a 'view' action is rejected when follow is false (manual pin). So the C1 promotion through reducePresentation(focused, {type:'view',view:'chart'}, source) respects manual override: if the user had manually pinned a view (follow:false), the promotion is refused (recorded as rejected receipt). That matches "respecting existing follow/manual-override semantics". 

Note: in handleToggleComponent, `focused` is built with `{...reducePresentation(...), selectedTargets, panel:'selection'}` — overrides selectedTargets/panel after the reducer. Then fromInset ? reducePresentation(focused, {type:'view',view:'chart'}, source) : focused. Wait — one subtlety: `focused` sets panel:'selection' and selectedTargets directly (bypassing reducer), which matches the pre-existing behavior (the old line did exactly that). Then the view action is applied through the reducer. When view==='atelier' (default follow:true), follow stays true (view action doesn't change follow). So after promotion, follow remains true — meaning subsequent automatic view cues (from speech playback, e.g., presentationCues with view actions) can still change the view. That's "existing follow semantics" preserved.

Hmm, but wait — one more check on the revert handler in App (line ~755-760):

```tsx
onEducationalSettled={() => {
  const lesson = insetLesson.current;
  insetLesson.current = null;
  if (!lesson || lesson.manual) return;
  setPresentation(previous => previous.view === 'chart' ? reducePresentation(previous,{type:'view',view:'atelier'},lesson.chart) : previous);
}}
```

If follow was false (manual pin) at promotion time, the promotion was rejected, view !== 'chart' → revert is a no-op (previous.view === 'chart' check). Good — idempotent and safe.

But hold on — what if follow is false and view is 'chart' because the user manually chose chart earlier (setManualView('chart') via Astrolabe onActivate)? Then insetLesson... wait, in that case fromInset would be false (view already 'chart'), so insetLesson never set → revert no-op. Good.

What if user manually chooses 'chart' via setManualView DURING the lesson? manual=true → no revert. Good — "manual choice cancels auto-revert".

What if user manually chooses 'atelier' during the lesson? manual=true → no revert (already atelier anyway). Good.

Now — an edge case: setManualView marks insetLesson.current.manual = true even when the manual view choice is unrelated... any setManualView during a lesson cancels auto-revert. That's exactly the clause: "UNLESS the user manually chose a view during the lesson". 

